  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Carved Chest",
    "overview": "Sandyq means chest — the carved wooden sandyq where a Kazakh family keeps what is valuable, each thing in its place. The system borrows that object's discipline rather than its ornament: almost square, carved corners (2px on every control), flat surfaces laid out in order, thin gold lines where a craftsman would cut an edge, and one wine-red mark for the thing that matters most on the screen. It is a working tool first. Cashiers take orders at a table in a full hall; the chest keeps everything findable, calm and within reach, and lets the brand show in precise details instead of decoration.\n\nThe world is warm but restrained: cream working areas, white cards, ink text, a dark ink frame (the POS header, the office sidebar, the sign-in screen) that holds the working area like a chest's lid and body. Cormorant, the brand's display serif, is kept for titles and prices; everything operational is set in Golos Text so Kazakh and Russian read equally well at a glance. Density is honest to the surface: the POS and kitchen are touch-first with 48–56px targets, the office is a desktop tool with a compact mode.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Carved, almost-square forms: 2px controls, 6px cards, 8px panels; never pill buttons.",
      "Flat by default: tonal layers (cream → white card) and hairline borders; shadows only where a surface floats.",
      "One wine primary per screen; gold is line and ornament, brass is the readable accent.",
      "Cormorant for titles and prices with tabular figures, Golos Text for everything else.",
      "Day and night themes over the same semantic tokens; contrast is checked in CI.",
      "The brand lives in details: the 4px rust heading accent, gold active markers, category strips."
    ],
